  "query_preview": "..Strong east winds have been persistent through the \nmorning into early afternoon hours, especially for the locales that \nare most susceptible to east winds. KTUS had a couple of max wind \ngusts in the 9 AM and 10 AM hours of 64 MPH. Elsewhere, we're seeing \ngusts in the 30-50 mph range in locations most prone to east winds. \nThe trends have certainly decreased somewhat in the last couple of \nhours and while scattered areas will still see windy conditions with \ngusts around 40 mph, it's not going to be widespread. Thus, we've \nlet the Wind Advisory expire. With that said, the east winds will \nstill be with us and those breezy winds (around 15 to 25 mph with \nlocal gusts 30 to 40 mph for prone locations) will be with us into \nSunday morning. \n\nOutside of the wind concerns, we're monitoring an upper level low \nnear 31N/119W or just off the northern Baja coast. Southerly flow \naround this feature continues to result in some scattered shower \nactivity (and even a few embedded thunderstorms) west of Tucson \nmoving from south to north. This upper low will eject northeast and \ntransition into an open wave on Sunday across Arizona. With our area \nremaining in southerly flow east of the trough axis, moisture levels \nwill remain elevated and the chance of showers with a potential \nrumble or two of thunder will continue with each minor disturbance \nmoving through tonight and Sunday. PoPs are currently in the 20 to \n50 percent range with rainfall amounts generally less than 0.25\" for \nthose locations that see rain tonight through Sunday.\n\nWhile the aforementioned system shears out to our northeast Monday \nevening, it will be replaced by an upper level low and longwave \ntrough off the California coast on Tuesday. This trough will move \nonshore Thursday and into the Great Basin Friday with southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow continuing for us ahead of the actual trough. \nThis will bring us a continuation of unsettled weather and rain \nchances (10 to 40 percent) Monday and Tuesday with a ramp up into \nthe 30 to 70 percent range Wednesday and Thursday ahead of the \ntrough axis, with some lingering showers possible Friday as well. \n\nTemperatures through the forecast period will generally be slightly \nbelow normal, though near normal temperatures will occur around \nTuesday and Wednesday.",
